          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A patient presents with progressive nasal obstruction affecting only the right s</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient presents with progressive nasal obstruction affecting only the right side. Based on the laterality principle, which category of cause is most likely?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Allergic mucosal disease</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Allergic rhinitis causes bilateral obstruction because the nasal mucosa on both sides is equally exposed to the inhaled allergen.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Structural or neoplastic pathology</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Unilateral nasal obstruction is the hallmark of structural causes (deviated septum, polyp) or neoplastic pathology. Laterality is the single most powerful discriminator in evaluating nasal obstruction.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Vasomotor autonomic dysfunction</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Vasomotor rhinitis produces bilateral symptoms triggered by physical stimuli such as cold air or perfumes and does not localise to one side.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Atrophic rhinitis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Atrophic rhinitis affects both nasal cavities symmetrically, producing wide passages with crusting and anosmia bilaterally.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the laterality rule: unilateral obstruction points to structural or neoplastic pathology, while bilateral obstruction suggests mucosal inflammatory disease."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly right. Unilateral obstruction demands investigation for structural and neoplastic causes — this single question narrows the differential more than any other."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
