          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 6-year-old boy has chronic mouth breathing, snoring, and bilateral conductive </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 6-year-old boy has chronic mouth breathing, snoring, and bilateral conductive hearing loss of 30 dB. Anterior rhinoscopy is entirely normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Bilateral nasal polyps</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Nasal polyps would be visible on anterior rhinoscopy and are uncommon in young children without cystic fibrosis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Deviated nasal septum</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A deviated septum causes unilateral, not bilateral, obstruction and would be visible on anterior examination.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Adenoid hypertrophy</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Normal anterior rhinoscopy with nasal obstruction and conductive hearing loss in a child points to adenoid hypertrophy. Enlarged adenoids obstruct the posterior choanae and compress the Eustachian tube openings, causing glue ear.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Allergic rhinitis with turbinate oedema</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Allergic rhinitis produces visible turbinate congestion on anterior rhinoscopy and does not typically cause isolated conductive hearing loss in this pattern.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember: normal anterior rhinoscopy plus nasal obstruction in a young child means the obstruction is posterior. Adenoid hypertrophy obstructs the choanae and compresses the Eustachian tubes, causing otitis media with effusion."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. The key pearl: impressive nasal symptoms with a completely normal anterior rhinoscopy means the pathology lies posteriorly — adenoids in a child this age."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
