          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>An elderly hypertensive patient on aspirin presents with blood running down the </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>An elderly hypertensive patient on aspirin presents with blood running down the posterior pharyngeal wall and no visible anterior bleeding point. Which vessel is the most likely source?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Anterior ethmoidal artery</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The anterior ethmoidal artery contributes to Little's area on the anterior septum and typically produces forward-dripping anterior epistaxis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Superior labial artery</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The superior labial artery supplies the anterior nasal septum via Kiesselbach's plexus, producing anterior, not posterior, epistaxis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Greater palatine artery</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The greater palatine artery supplies the hard palate and anterior nasal floor. It contributes to the anterior septal blood supply, not posterior bleeds.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Sphenopalatine artery</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Posterior epistaxis arises from the sphenopalatine artery or Woodruff's plexus. Blood flows into the pharynx with no visible anterior source — the classic pattern in elderly hypertensive patients.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember the anterior versus posterior distinction: 90% of nosebleeds arise anteriorly from Little's area (Kiesselbach's plexus), but blood running down the throat with no anterior source indicates posterior epistaxis from the sphenopalatine artery."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly right. Posterior epistaxis from the sphenopalatine artery accounts for only 10% of nosebleeds but causes the most clinically serious cases. Always manage blood pressure and anticoagulants concurrently."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
