          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A patient with confirmed allergic rhinitis finds that oral cetirizine relieves s</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient with confirmed allergic rhinitis finds that oral cetirizine relieves sneezing and itch but has not improved persistent bilateral nasal congestion. Which drug class specifically targets the congestion that antihistamines cannot address?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Intranasal corticosteroids such as mometasone</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Allergic nasal congestion is driven by the late-phase response — cysteinyl leukotrienes recruit eosinophils, causing persistent turbinate oedema that antihistamines cannot suppress. Intranasal corticosteroids are first-line for this obstruction.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>First-generation antihistamines such as chlorpheniramine</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>First-generation antihistamines cross the blood-brain barrier and impair cognition. They still only target early-phase histamine effects and add no efficacy against late-phase congestion.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Intranasal anticholinergics such as ipratropium</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Ipratropium blocks glandular secretion and is indicated for rhinorrhoea-dominant vasomotor rhinitis, not allergic nasal congestion driven by eosinophilic turbinate oedema.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Oral decongestants such as pseudoephedrine</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Oral decongestants are not first-line for persistent allergic nasal obstruction. They provide temporary relief but carry cardiovascular side effects and do not address the underlying eosinophilic inflammation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember the two-phase model: the early phase (histamine) drives sneezing and itch, but the late phase (leukotrienes and eosinophils) drives persistent nasal congestion. Intranasal corticosteroids are first-line for this late-phase obstruction."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. The two-phase model predicts this: antihistamines control early-phase symptoms (sneezing, itch), but late-phase congestion requires intranasal corticosteroids used consistently over two to four weeks."
                }
              ],
